            ("h2", "Why nobody stopped it"),
            ("p", "Seven years is a long time to not stop something. It is worth being precise about why that happens, because the mechanism is the transferable part."),
            ("h3", "1. Every increment was locally rational"),
            ("p", "No one ever approved \"spend €500m and cancel.\" They approved a sequence of small, sensible-looking things: this integration, that reconciliation, one more quarter of stabilisation. Each was cheaper than stopping. That is the defining property of drift — at every single decision point, continuing is the cheaper option, right up until the whole thing is written off."),
            ("h3", "2. Reporting measured activity, not viability"),
            ("p", "Status reporting on programmes like this tracks deliverables completed, tickets closed, modules configured, milestones passed. All of those can be green while the answer to \"will this system run our largest markets at real volume?\" is quietly getting worse. Deployments reportedly worked in smaller markets; the larger, higher-intensity ones were where the customised design ran out of road."),
            ("h3", "3. The escalation path had a filter on it"),
            ("p", "Engineers raised architecture and scalability concerns. The gap between what dashboards showed and what practitioners saw kept widening. Signals that survive four layers of \"let's not escalate that yet\" arrive as adjectives, not as decisions. This is the same structural problem described in <a href=\"../infrastructure-inbreeding/\">infrastructure inbreeding</a>: when the layer raising the alarm and the layer receiving it share assumptions and incentives, the alarm becomes noise."),
            ("h3", "4. Sunk cost had a face"),
            ("p", "By year five, cancelling meant a named executive publicly owning a nine-figure write-off. The CEO left in 2017 amid reported strategy disagreements; senior IT leadership turned over. It is notably common that a programme becomes cancellable only after the people identified with it are gone — which is an organisational fact, not a technical one."),

            ("h2", "What Lidl actually got right"),
